hi this is preshtalwalker in this video  we're going to explain why gas stations  are often located next to each other  let's think about two hot dog stands  that are on a beach  each hot dog sand wants to be closest to  the most customers  we can think about the shoreline as  being a straight line that goes from one  end to the other  if the blue and green hot dog stands  spread out evenly across the beach  then the blue stand will get all the  customers to the left of the middle  and the green stand will get all the  customers to the right of the middle  this location of the two stands is very  good for everyone involved  the two stands spread the beach evenly  and they're closest  to customers on both sides  customers left of center can go to the  blue stand and customers right at the  center can go to the green stand  however  each stand wants to locate to be closer  to more customers  so the green stand thinks  what happens if i move closer to the  blue stand  when the green stand moves closer  it actually captures some of the  customers that were going to the blue  stand  so now the green stand has more than  half of the beach because it's closer to  more customers  the blue stand thinks about the  situation and decides that it can get  its customers back by moving closer to  the center as well  so the blue stand moves over to the  center  and it regains some of its customers  as you can see the blue and green stands  both want to move closer to the center  so that they can get more customers and  be closer  to customers than the other stand  ultimately they both end up at the  center  and at this point they're equally close  to all the customers that are along the  shoreline  so they split the customers evenly  and there's an interesting thing at this  point is that neither stand is going to  want to move  if either stand moves to the left or the  right  then that stand is going to get less  than half the customers  so this is the solution to the game  and this is the reason why businesses  often locate right next to each other  when competing on location  everyone wants the central location to  be as close to as many customers as  possible  this is true for gas stations it's also  true for fast food chains  and it's even true for political  candidates  during election campaigns  they all go to the center position to  seek out the average vote  so although we would be better served if  the stands and businesses were spread  out  because of competition they all moved to  the center  i hope you like this video please  subscribe to my channel  you can catch me on my blog mind your  decisions or on twitter at  preshtalwalker  and if you like this video please buy my  book on game theory  this example of gas stations is the  first chapter in my book you can get my  book in paperback and ebook form at  amazon.com  it's called the joy of game theory an  introduction to strategic thinking
 

